#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# eliminate

#% text
# `eliminate(eqs, xs)` gives the polynomial equations `p == 0` implied by the equations `eqs` that are free of the unknowns `xs`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The equations are polynomial; the result comes from a Groebner basis with the unknowns `xs` ordered first.

#% item
# The result describes the projection of the solution set; it may be larger than the set of points that have a solution.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
eliminate([x + y == 3, x - y == 1], [y])
#% output
#= -2 + x == 0

#% cell
eliminate([x == t^2, y == t^3], [t])
#% output
#= -y^2 + x^3 == 0

#% cell
eliminate([x == cos_t, y == sin_t, cos_t^2 + sin_t^2 == 1], [cos_t, sin_t])
#% output
#= -1 + x^2 + y^2 == 0

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The implicit equation of the parametrized curve `x = t^2 - 1`, `y = t^3 - t`:

#% cell
eliminate([x == t^2 - 1, y == t^3 - t], [t])
#% output
#= -y^2 + x^2 + x^3 == 0

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The same as the polynomials of a Groebner basis free of the eliminated variables:

#% cell
groebner_basis([x - t^2, y - t^3], [t, x, y])
#% output
#= [-y^2 + x^3, -x^2 + t*y, -y + t*x, -x + t^2]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `groebner_basis`, `resultant`, `solve`
